From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 01:30:26 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Route winemetal to a remote Metal daemon behind a runtime
 flag

The device this runs on has no usable GPU feature set: it reports no Metal
family at all and no BC support, so texture formats are remapped and shaders
are compiled for a machine that will not execute them. This routes the
winemetal unixcall surface to a Metal daemon on another host instead, decided
once per process and off unless configured.

Which calls travel, and which deliberately do not:

- Descriptors cross VERBATIM as their WMT*Info structs. Copying them field by
  field into a parallel schema is what let an earlier pipeline path become a
  toy: eight colour attachments, blend factors, write masks, topology and
  tessellation simply were not carried, and nothing said so.
- Buffers keep the CALLER'S memory as their storage. A caller-supplied
  memory.ptr is what the ring allocator writes argument data through;
  substituting an allocation meant the app wrote one block while the flush
  uploaded another, so shaders read zero addresses and every draw produced
  nothing while the clear still landed.
- Shader bytes cross, containers do not. DispatchData, the on-disk cache,
  autorelease pools, event listeners and strings are guest-local by design;
  routing them put untagged guest pointers on the wire.
- Calls whose iOS implementation is a no-op that WRITES ITS OUTPUTS stay
  local. Guarding them was worse than running them: the guard returns without
  touching outputs that are not named `ret`, and one of those is queried once
  per frame, so a display version read from uninitialised stack made the
  renderer reconfigure presentation several times a second.

Handles carry a tag. A local pointer reaching the wire, or a host id reaching
a local call, is then a named error at the point of misuse rather than a
subtly wrong frame on another machine. Lifetime calls dispatch on that tag,
because guest-local objects legitimately exist in this mode.

The guard table is GENERATED, not hand-written. ntdll indexes the dispatch
table directly, so there is no central hook and the guard has to be the table
entry; the slot number is the ABI, and one inserted line would send every
later call to the wrong function.

Unrouted calls fail by name. Discovering the surface by running is what
worked: a local census of a rendering client showed 62 of 127 entries in use,
against a much larger guess.

